"Separates internal strengths and weaknesses from external opportunities and threats relevant to a defined strategic objective.\n\nUse when: Build a shared initial view of a strategic situation. Collect evidence before generating and evaluating strategic options.\n\nAvoid when: The audience expects the four lists alone to prescribe an executable strategy.\n\nVisual grammar: Four labeled regions cross internal versus external factors with helpful versus harmful effects. Brief evidence-backed statements occupy the corresponding region. References and uncertainty marks stay attached to claims.\n\nBuild: State the objective, scope and assessment horizon. Gather evidence and classify factors by controllability and effect. Prioritize material factors and carry them into explicit option generation.\n\nCheck: Internal capabilities are distinct from external conditions. Every factor relates to the stated strategic objective. A factor's interpretation includes its context and evidence.\n\nPitfalls: Filling quadrants with generic slogans to achieve visual symmetry. Listing desired actions as existing strengths or opportunities.\n\nExample: A museum reviewing membership growth separates strong volunteer support, limited opening hours, nearby development and competing leisure offers.\n\nAlternatives: TOWS matrix, PESTLE analysis."
